Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 13:47:03 +0000
Subject: fix: support pending trusted objects in snapshot serialization

ObjectSerializer::Serialize() may defer a trusted object such as a
RegExpData when it is first reached at the maximum recursion depth. The
object is then registered as pending and a forward reference is emitted
through the trusted pointer slot. A later trusted pointer to the same
object, e.g. from a second JSRegExp that shares the RegExpData through
the compilation cache, hit the CHECK in VisitTrustedPointer() which
assumed pending objects can never be seen there, aborting
node_mksnapshot.

The deserializer already supports pending forward references through
trusted pointer slots: ReadRegisterPendingForwardRef() records the
trusted pointer reference descriptor, and SerializePrologue() emits
kInitializeSelfTrustedPointer before resolving the pending object. So
emit a forward reference for pending objects, the same way
VisitPointers() does for tagged slots.

https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/v8/v8/+/8559539 changed
DescriptorArray sharing, which reshaped the startup snapshot object
graph enough for a shared RegExpData to be deferred and then referenced
again.

This should be upstreamed.

diff --git a/src/snapshot/serializer.cc b/src/snapshot/serializer.cc
index 22d526156392f70e85a7fb4b9b2a38d517f17ff2..5b5695d42249831eed984f25dbec415e6df9d826 100644
--- a/src/snapshot/serializer.cc
+++ b/src/snapshot/serializer.cc
@@ -1354,10 +1354,14 @@ void Serializer::ObjectSerializer::VisitTrustedPointer(
   CHECK(IsHeapObject(*slot_value));
   bytes_processed_so_far_ += kTrustedPointerSize;
 
-  // Currently we cannot see pending objects here, but we may need to support
-  // them in the future. They should already be supported by the deserializer.
-  CHECK(!serializer_->SerializePendingObject(*slot_value));
   sink_->Put(kTrustedPointerPrefix, "TrustedPointer");
+  // The referenced object may already be pending if an earlier reference to
+  // it was deferred by ObjectSerializer::Serialize(), e.g. a RegExpData shared
+  // by several JSRegExp objects that was first reached at the maximum
+  // recursion depth. Emit a forward reference in that case, as VisitPointers()
+  // does for tagged slots; the deserializer resolves it through the slot's
+  // reference descriptor, which already records the trusted pointer prefix.
+  if (serializer_->SerializePendingObject(*slot_value)) return;
   serializer_->SerializeObject(slot_value, SlotType::kAnySlot);
 #else
   UNREACHABLE();
